      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment. It's pages 4-5 of OIML Certificate R 137/2012-B-NL1-18.04, project number 1901860, from the Netherlands (NMi).\n\nThe fragment contains:\n1. Certificate metadata (number, project number, page 4 of 5 and 5 of 5, member state)\n2. Table 2 Continued, FMR-Dual (interesting — header says \"FMG-Dual\" in the table title but the table header says \"FMR-Dual\") — a matrix table of rotary gas meter variants with Volume (cyclic volume in dm³), G-value, Qmax, minimum Qmin, Qt, maximum pmax (bar), Diameter D (mm), Accuracy class.\n3. Footnotes: * about cyclic volume on name plate; ** about low pressure (21 bar) or high pressure (101 bar) variant.\n4. Table 3 Register — meter size, minimum number of drums before/behind the comma, control-element [m³].\n5. Interchangeable components note: mechanical index with reed contact, Wiegand or encoder.\n6. Installation conditions: not applicable.\n\nNote the G-values: some rows have \"160\", \"250\", \"400\", \"650\" without the \"G\" prefix — these are OCR drops, but I should extract as given. Actually per rule \"Don't fabricate\" — but the values clearly are G-values; the \"G\" prefix is missing. I think it's reasonable to keep them as-is (\"160\") or normalize? The rule says don't fabricate. But interpreting the table, they are G-values. I'll keep them as strings exactly as shown: \"G40\", \"G65\", \"G100\", \"G160\", \"G250\", \"400\", etc. Hmm, but for model_family models, I could list distinct G-values: G40, G65, G100, G160, G250, G400, G650, G1000. The rows without \"G\" prefix (160, 250, 400, 650) likely correspond to G160, G250, G400, G650. Should I normalize? Risky but reasonable. I think I'll keep the raw values in matrix table rows but normalize in model family to G-values that appear. Actually, to be safe, I'll preserve as-is in the matrix table, and for model_family, list the distinct G-values as they appear, noting normalized ones... Hmm.\n\nLet me think. The certificate is for a family of rotary gas meters FMR-Dual (and presumably FMG-Dual on earlier pages, but this fragment only has the FMR-Dual continuation). The fragment only includes this content, so I should extract what's here.\n\nModel family: models could be identified by G-value. Distinct G-values: G40, G65, G100, G160, G250, G400, G650, G1000. But there are also volume variants: multiple rows share the same G-value with different cyclic volumes (e.g., G160 at 1.22, 1.46, 1.98, 2.32 dm³; G250 at 1.98, 2.32, 2.90). So a model is really a combination of cyclic volume and G-value. The best model_id might be like \"V=1.22/G160\" etc.\n\nGiven the schema, I'd construct models from table rows: each row is a variant with cyclic volume + G-value + diameter. There are 24 rows (each rowspan=2 or 3). Let me enumerate:\n\n1. V=0.50, G40, Qmax=65, Qmin=0.4, Qt=3.2, pmax=21/101, D=50 or 80, class 1.0 or 1.5\n2. V=0.50, G65, Qmax=100, Qmin=0.65, Qt=5, pmax, D=50 or 80\n3. V=0.78, G65, Qmax=100, Qmin=0.65, Qt=5, D=50 or 80\n4. V=0.78, G100, Qmax=160, Qmin=1, Qt=8, D=80 or 100\n5. V=1.22, G100, 160, 1, 8, D=80 or 100\n6. V=1.22, G160, 250, 1.6, 12.5, D=80 or 100\n7. V=1.98, G160, 250, 1.6, 12.5, D=80 or 100\n8. V=1.98, G250, 400, 2.5, 20, D=80 or 100 or 150\n9. V=1.46, G100, 160, 1, 8, D=80 or 100\n10. V=1.46, G160, 250, 1.6, 12.5, D=80 or 100\n11. V=2.32, G160, 250, 2.5, 12.5, D=80 or 100\n12. V=2.32, G250, 400, 4, 20, D=80 or 100 or 150\n13. V=2.41, \"160\" (G160), 250, 1, 12.5, D=80 or 100\n14. V=2.41, \"250\" (G250), 400, 2.5, 20, D=80 or 100\n15. V=2.90, G250, 400, 2.5, 20, D=80 or 100 or 150\n16. V=2.90, G400, 650, 4, 32, D=100 or 150\n17. V=3.96, \"250\" (G250), 400, 2.5, 20, D=100 or 150\n18. V=3.96, \"400\" (G400), 650, 4, 32, D=100 or 150\n19. V=3.96, \"650\" (G650), 1000, 4, 32, D=150 or 200\n20. V=6.34, \"400\" (G400), 650, 4, 32, D=150 or 200\n21. V=6.34, \"650\" (G650), 1000, 6.5, 50, D=150 or 200\n22. V=10.3, G650, 1000, 6.5, 50, D=150 or 200\n23. V=10.3, G1000, 1600, 10, 80, D=150 or 200\n\nWait, rows: 0.50 has rowspan 2 (G40, G65) — 2 rows. 0.78 rowspan 2 (G65, G100) — 2. 1.22 rowspan 2 (G100, G160) — 2. 1.98 rowspan 2 (G160, G250) — 2. 1.46 rowspan 2 (G100, G160) — 2. 2.32 rowspan 2 (G160, G250) — 2. 2.41 rowspan 2 (160, 250) — 2. 2.90 rowspan 2 (G250, G400) — 2. 3.96 rowspan 3 (250, 400, 650) — 3. 6.34 rowspan 2 (400, 650) — 2. 10.3 rowspan 2 (G650, G1000) — 2. Total = 2+2+2+2+2+2+2+2+3+2+2 = 23 rows.\n\nFor the matrix table, I'll preserve the G-values as-is (with \"G\" prefix where present, without where missing). Hmm, but that creates inconsistency. I think it's fine to normalize — the OCR dropped the \"G\". The instruction says don't fabricate, but normalizing \"160\" → \"G160\" in a G-value column is a reasonable interpretation, not fabrication. However, to be conservative and traceable, maybe I should keep raw. Let me decide: I'll normalize to G-prefixed form since the column is explicitly \"G-value\" and it's clearly an OCR artifact. Actually the schema says rows[] object per row; the value in the G column — I'll write \"G160\" etc. I think that's the intent of extraction. I'll do that.\n\nDecimal conversion: 0,50 → 0.5; 0,4 → 0.4; 1,0 or 1,5 → accuracy class options \"1.0 or 1.5\" — could be a list of alternatives. I'll represent as string \"1.0 or 1.5\" or as list [1.0, 1.5]. Schema rows are object per row; values can be numbers or strings. Diameter \"50 or 80\" — string. Accuracy class \"1.0 or 1.5\" — string. pmax \"21 / 101\" — string (two variants: low pressure 21 bar, high pressure 101 bar). Footnote marker \"**\" applies to pmax column.\n\nColumns: cyclic_volume (V, dm³), g_value, qmax (m³/h), qmin_minimum (m³/h), qt (m³/h), pmax_maximum (bar), diameter (mm), accuracy_class.\n\nNote \"minimum Qmin\" and \"maximum pmax\" — these are minimum/maximum limits: Qmin stated is the minimum allowed, pmax the maximum allowed. For the matrix rows I'll just record the values.\n\nModel family: The models here — the model_id could be the G-value combined with cyclic volume. The R137 example in the prompt used size as model_id. Here, multiple rows per G-value (different cyclic volumes and Qmin values). I'll create model_id as \"G160/V2.32\" style or just list G-values? To be precise, I'll create models with model_id like \"G40 V0.5\" ... maybe \"G40 (V=0.5 dm³)\". Let me use model_id \"G40_V0.50\" etc. Or simpler: since the schema example used size, I could use combined id. I'll do model_id: \"G160-2.32dm³\"? Hmm, keep it simple and ASCII: \"G160 V2.32\".\n\nActually let me reconsider: perhaps model entries should be per row of table 2. That's 23 models. That's fine.\n\nmodel_level characteristics: qmax, qmin (minimum), qt, pmax (maximum), cyclic_volume, diameter, accuracy_class — these vary per model. I could add model_level entries for qmax, qt, qmin at least. pmax is constant \"21/101\" across all — that's config-ish (low/high pressure variant). Diameter varies. Cyclic volume varies.\n\nGiven the fragment, I'll populate:\n- matrix_tables: two tables (Table 2 FMR-Dual flow characteristics, Table 3 Register).\n- model_family: models per table-2 row.\n- characteristics.model_level: qmax, minimum_qmin, qt, cyclic_volume, maximum_pmax? pmax value is \"21 / 101\" string — it's a choice between variants, better as config or footnote. I'll record pmax with footnote marker \"2)\" and value \"21 or 101\" string in matrix rows; in model_level maybe skip since constant-ish but it's per-model anyway... All rows have same pmax, so it's type-level with alternatives? Actually it's a variant choice (low pressure vs high pressure), not varying by model. I could put it in type_level as max_pressure with value {list} — schema says single value... The structured value shape allows string. I'd put type_level: maximum_pressure: value \"21 or 101 (low/high pressure variant)\"? Hmm.\n\nBetter: config_level with axis \"pressure_variant\": conditions \"low pressure\" → 21 bar, \"high pressure\" → 101 bar, with footnote marker \"2)\". That matches rule 5 nicely.\n\n- characteristics.type_level: accuracy_class? It's \"1.0 or 1.5\" for all models — constant. Could be type_level with value \"1.0 or 1.5\" — but it's really a list. I'll put accuracy_class per model in matrix and also in model_level? All same value; type_level could note accuracy_class {value: \"1.0 or 1.5\"}. Hmm, rule 2 says lists of values (Emax multiple) → model matrix. Here every model has both options. I'll put it in matrix rows only, and maybe type_level accuracy_class with footnote? Let me include in matrix rows as string \"1.0 or 1.5\" and skip type_level. Also certificate-level accuracy_classes in recommendation: R137 doesn't define accuracy classes like that; but gas meters classes are 1.0 and 1.5. The recommendation object: id \"R137\", edition 2012, scheme \"B\". accuracy_classes [1.0, 1.5]? The certificate number says R 137/2012. Scheme B (from \"B\" in number: R 137/2012-B-NL1-18.04). accuracy_classes: [\"1.0\", \"1.5\"] — the table shows class 1.0 or 1.5. I'll include [1.0, 1.5].\n\n- footnotes: two from page 4 (single * and double **). Marker \"1)\" and \"2)\"? They're written as \"\\*\" and \"\\*\\*\". I'll use markers \"*\" and \"**\" or convert to \"1)\" \"2)\"? Schema example uses \"1)\". The markdown shows asterisks. I'll keep \"*\" and \"**\" as markers, and reference \"**\" in pmax values' footnote_markers, \"*\" in cyclic_volume footnote markers.\n\nFootnote 1 text: \"On the name plate of the rotary meter the cyclic volume can be given in two possible formats: 1. with two digits behind the comma as stated in table 2 and 3, or 2. with a number containing 6 significant digits. In this case a HF pulse value can be accurately derived from the spinning rotors with an optical sensor.\"\n\nFootnote 2 text: \"The FMR-Dual can be delivered as a low pressure (Pmax = 21 bar) or a high pressure (Pmax = 101 bar) variant.\"\n\n- Components: \"The mechanical index, equipped with a reed contact, Wiegand or encoder, is an interchangeable component.\" → components entry: role \"interchangeable component\", type_designations [\"mechanical index\"], characteristics? Equipped with reed contact, Wiegand or encoder → alternatives. I'll add characteristics: {\"pickup\": {\"alternatives\": [\"reed contact\", \"Wiegand\", \"encoder\"]}}? The schema characteristics{} is free. I'll do: role: \"interchangeable_component\", type_designations: [\"mechanical index\"], alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics: {\"reading_device_options\": ...}. Keep simple.\n\n- Installation conditions: \"For this rotary meter specific installation conditions are not applicable.\" Where to put? Maybe in certified_type.description or characteristics.type_level installation_conditions: \"not applicable\". I'll add certified_type description summarizing: \"Rotary gas meters FMR-Dual (Table 2 continued)...\" Hmm — certified_type isn't explicitly in fragment but the fragment is about FMR-Dual rotary meter. The category: \"Gas meter\" (rotary meter / rotary displacement meter? R137 is gas meters). type_designations: \"FMR-Dual\" (also title mentions FMG-Dual — \"Table 2 Continued, FMG-Dual\" but table header FMR-Dual; likely typo; the previous page presumably had FMG-Dual). I'll include type_designations [\"FMR-Dual\"] and maybe mention FMG-Dual in matrix table name. Since fragment says \"Table 2 Continued, FMG-Dual\" heading with table header \"FMR-Dual\" — I'll record table name as given: \"Table 2 Continued, FMG-Dual (table header: FMR-Dual)\". Hmm, simpler: name \"Table 2 – FMR-Dual (continued)\". The instruction: preserve. I'll use name \"Table 2 Continued, FMG-Dual\" and description \"Rotary displacement meter FMR-Dual flow characteristics (continued)\".\n\nPage total: 5. Certificate number: \"R 137/2012-B-NL1-18.04\". Member state: Netherlands. Issuing authority: NMi (Netherlands Measuring Institute) — the header shows \"NMi⁺\". Name \"NMi\" — I could include issuing_authority.name \"NMi (Netherlands Measuring Institute)\"? Don't fabricate address. I'll include name \"NMi\" and oiml_issuer_id maybe \"NL1\"? The number contains \"NL1\" which is the NMi issuer ID. Hmm, oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"? I think including \"NL1\" is reasonable as it's part of the number. I'll include it in certificate.oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\". Actually the fragment doesn't explicitly say issuer id. But the certificate number format R 137/2012-B-NL1-18.04 includes NL1. I'll set certificate.oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\". Safe enough. Or omit. I'll include — it's clearly encoded.\n\ndate_issued: not in fragment — omit.\n\nTable 3 Register: columns meter_size, min_drums_before_comma, min_drums_behind_comma, control_element (m³). Rows:\n- G6: 5, 3, 0.0002\n- G10 – G65: 6, 2, 0.002\n- G100 – G650: 7, 1, 0.02\n\nNow model_level characteristics: let me build:\n\nmodels (23):\n1. G40 V0.50\n2. G65 V0.50\n3. G65 V0.78\n4. G100 V0.78\n5. G100 V1.22\n6. G160 V1.22\n7. G160 V1.98\n8. G250 V1.98\n9. G100 V1.46\n10. G160 V1.46\n11. G160 V2.32\n12. G250 V2.32\n13. G160 V2.41\n14. G250 V2.41\n15. G250 V2.90\n16. G400 V2.90\n17. G250 V3.96\n18. G400 V3.96\n19. G650 V3.96\n20. G400 V6.34\n21. G650 V6.34\n22. G650 V10.3\n23. G1000 V10.3\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- cyclic_volume (dm³): values per model (0.5, 0.5, 0.78, 0.78, 1.22, 1.22, 1.98, 1.98, 1.46, 1.46, 2.32, 2.32, 2.41, 2.41, 2.9, 2.9, 3.96, 3.96, 3.96, 6.34, 6.34, 10.3, 10.3), footnote marker \"*\".\n- g_value: string values per model. Hmm, g_value is part of model_id; maybe skip model_level for it. I'll skip since it's embedded in model_id.\n- qmax (m³/h): 65, 100, 100, 160, 160, 250, 250, 400, 160, 250, 250, 400, 250, 400, 400, 650, 400, 650, 1000, 650, 1000, 1000, 1600.\n- minimum_qmin (m³/h): 0.4, 0.65, 0.65, 1, 1, 1.6, 1.6, 2.5, 1, 1.6, 2.5, 4, 1, 2.5, 2.5, 4, 2.5, 4, 4, 4, 4, 6.5, 10.\n- qt (m³/h): 3.2, 5, 5, 8, 8, 12.5, 12.5, 20, 8, 12.5, 12.5, 20, 12.5, 20, 20, 32, 20, 32, 32, 32, 32, 50, 80.\n- maximum_pmax (bar): constant \"21 / 101\" with \"**\" footnote — but as config? pmax isn't tied to model but to variant. I'll make config_level for pressure variant instead, and in matrix rows keep \"21 / 101\" string with footnote marker \"**\".\n- diameter (mm): 50 or 80, 50 or 80, 50 or 80, 80 or 100, 80 or 100, 80 or 100, 80 or 100, \"80 or 100 or 150\", 80 or 100, 80 or 100, 80 or 100, \"80 or 100 or 150\", 80 or 100, 80 or 100, \"80 or 100 or 150\", \"100 or 150\", \"100 or 150\", \"100 or 150\", \"150 or 200\", \"150 or 200\", \"150 or 200\", \"150 or 200\", \"150 or 200\". Strings.\n- accuracy_class: \"1.0 or 1.5\" all.\n\nShould model_level include diameter and accuracy_class? They vary in alternatives form (strings). The schema example included only numeric varying attributes in model_level. I'll include diameter too as strings — it varies. Accuracy class constant \"1.0 or 1.5\" — I could put in type_level: accuracy_class {value \"1.0 or 1.5\"}. Hmm, that's a list-as-string; acceptable? Rule says lists of numeric values → model matrix; here it's constant alternatives. I'll put accuracy_class in matrix rows only and skip type_level to avoid misrepresenting. Actually including in type_level as string \"1.0 or 1.5\" seems informative. I'll include it in matrix rows; skip type_level. Hmm. Let me include type_level accuracy_class: {value: \"1.0 or 1.5\"} — no, keep clean: matrix only.\n\nConfig level: axis \"pressure_variant\": [{condition \"low pressure\", value 21, unit \"bar\", footnote \"**\"}, {condition \"high pressure\", value 101, unit \"bar\", footnote \"**\"}] attribute \"maximum_pressure\" (pmax).\n\nMatrix table 1:\nname: \"Table 2 (continued) – FMR-Dual flow characteristics\"\ncolumns: [\"cyclic_volume\", \"g_value\", \"qmax\", \"minimum_qmin\", \"qt\", \"maximum_pmax\", \"diameter\", \"accuracy_class\"]\ncolumn_units: [\"dm³\", null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"bar\", \"mm\", null]\nrows: 23 objects with values as described; footnote markers on cyclic_volume \"*\" and pmax \"**\". But schema rows are plain objects; footnotes[] at table level can note which columns carry markers. I'll add \"footnotes\" field at table level: [{\"marker\": \"*\", \"applies_to\": \"cyclic_volume\", ...}] Hmm schema says footnotes[] per matrix_table. I'll list markers.\n\nActually schema: matrix_tables: {name, columns[], rows[], description, footnotes[]}. I'll include column_units as in the example (they did \"column_units\" in example though schema yaml doesn't list it; the example includes it, so I'll include).\n\nFor g_value normalization: rows 13,14: \"160\"→\"G160\", \"250\"→\"G250\"; rows 17-19: \"250\"→\"G250\", \"400\"→\"G400\", \"650\"→\"G650\"; rows 20,21: \"400\"→\"G400\", \"650\"→\"G650\". I'll normalize and mention in description that OCR dropped \"G\" prefix. Good—transparency.\n\nValues numeric conversion: V 0,50→0.5; 0,78; 1,22; 1,98; 1,46; 2,32; 2,41; 2,90→2.9; 3,96; 6,34; 10,3. Qmin: 0,4→0.4; 0,65; 1; 1,6→1.6; 2,5→2.5; 4; 6,5→6.5; 10. Qt: 3,2→3.2; 5; 8; 12,5→12.5; 20; 32; 50; 80. pmax \"21 / 101\" string. Diameter strings. Accuracy \"1,0 or 1,5\"→\"1.0 or 1.5\".\n\nNow certificate object:\n```\n\"certificate\": {\n  \"number\": \"R 137/2012-B-NL1-18.04\",\n  \"scheme\": \"B\",\n  \"project_number\": \"1901860\",\n  \"page_total\": 5,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n```\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\" (from logo \"NMi⁺\" — I'll write \"NMi\"). person etc. absent → omit.\n\ncertified_type:\ncategory: \"Gas meter\" (rotary displacement gas meter). type_designations: [\"FMR-Dual\"]. Maybe also \"FMG-Dual\" mentioned in the table title. The title \"Table 2 Continued, FMG-Dual\" — probably refers to previous page's FMG-Dual table; this page continues with FMR-Dual. I'll include both? Risk of conflation. I'll set type_designations [\"FMR-Dual\", \"FMG-Dual\"]? Hmm. The certificate (from page 4 title) covers FMG-Dual (continued) and FMR-Dual tables. Table 2 apparently includes both FMG-Dual and FMR-Dual sections (page 3 would have FMG